narrative:
## Building a Mini Analytics System

A data pipeline processes events, enriches them with lookup data, and tracks unique values — all using different collections working in concert.

### The Domain

```xiom
type Event = {
  event_type: Str;
  user_id: Int;
  value: Int;
}

type Analytics = {
  events: Vec[Event];
  user_names: Map[Int, Str];
  unique_users: Set[Int];
}
```

Three collections, three purposes: Vec preserves order, Map provides lookups, Set guarantees uniqueness.

### Processing Events

```xiom
fn Analytics.new() -> Analytics {
  return Analytics{
    events: Vec[Event].new(),
    user_names: Map[Int, Str].new(),
    unique_users: Set[Int].new(),
  };
}

fn Analytics.record_event(a: &mut Analytics, event_type: Str, user_id: Int, value: Int) {
  let e = Event{ event_type: event_type, user_id: user_id, value: value };
  a.events.push(e);
  a.unique_users.insert(user_id);
}

fn Analytics.register_user(a: &mut Analytics, id: Int, name: Str) {
  a.user_names.insert(id, name);
}

fn Analytics.get_user_name(a: &Analytics, id: Int) -> Str {
  return match a.user_names.get(&id) {
    Some(name) => name,
    None => "Unknown",
  };
}

fn Analytics.total_events(a: &Analytics) -> Int {
  return a.events.len();
}

fn Analytics.unique_visitor_count(a: &Analytics) -> Int {
  // Count unique users who fired events
  return a.unique_users.len();
}
```

Hmm — Set doesn't have `.len()`. Let's track it manually:

```xiom
fn Analytics.unique_visitor_count(a: &Analytics, known_user_ids: &Vec[Int]) -> Int {
  // Count how many known users appear in events
  let count = 0;
  let i = 0;
  while i < known_user_ids.len() {
    let id = match known_user_ids.get(i) { Some(v) => v, None => 0 };
    if a.unique_users.contains(&id) {
      count = count + 1;
    }
    i = i + 1;
  }
  return count;
}
```

### Generating a Report

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  var ana: Analytics = Analytics.new();

  ana.register_user(&mut ana, 1, "Alice");
  ana.register_user(&mut ana, 2, "Bob");
  ana.register_user(&mut ana, 3, "Carol");

  ana.record_event(&mut ana, "click", 1, 100);
  ana.record_event(&mut ana, "view", 2, 50);
  ana.record_event(&mut ana, "click", 1, 200);
  ana.record_event(&mut ana, "purchase", 1, 500);

  io.println("Total events: " + string.int_to_str(ana.total_events()));
  io.println("User 1: " + ana.get_user_name(&ana, 1));
  return 0;
}
```

### Why Three Collections?

| Collection | Role | Why |
|-----------|------|-----|
| Vec[Event] | Event log | Preserves chronological order |
| Map[Int,Str] | User directory | Fast name lookup by ID |
| Set[Int] | Unique visitors | Automatic deduplication |

No single collection could do all three jobs. The art of data engineering is choosing the right collection for each job.

